---
source: cli/tests/help.rs
expression: text
---
Mount a btrfs image or block device read-only via FUSE

Usage: fuse [OPTIONS] <IMAGE> <MOUNTPOINT>

Arguments:
  <IMAGE>
          Path to the btrfs image file or block device

  <MOUNTPOINT>
          Mount point

Options:
  -f, --foreground
          Run in the foreground.
          
          The mount always runs in the foreground for now, so this flag has no
          effect.

      --allow-other
          Allow other users to access the mount

      --subvol <SUBVOL>
          Mount the named subvolume as the root of the FUSE filesystem.
          
          The path is relative to the filesystem root, for example --subvol
          home/snapshots/2025-01-01. Mutually exclusive with --subvolid.

      --subvolid <SUBVOLID>
          Mount the subvolume with this tree id.
          
          Use the value reported by btrfs subvolume list. Mutually exclusive
          with --subvol.

      --no-default-permissions
          Don't have the kernel check file mode and ownership against the
          calling user.
          
          By default the FUSE mount uses default_permissions, so a non-root user
          can't read root-owned files in the image, as with kernel btrfs. Pass
          this flag to skip that check, for example when inspecting an image
          whose stored ownership doesn't match your local UIDs.

      --cache-tree-blocks <CACHE_TREE_BLOCKS>
          Number of tree blocks to cache (~16 KiB each).
          
          Set to 1 to effectively disable the cache. Larger values trade RAM for
          fewer disk reads on tree walks.
          
          [default: 4096]

      --cache-inodes <CACHE_INODES>
          Number of parsed inode items to cache
          
          [default: 4096]

      --cache-extent-maps <CACHE_EXTENT_MAPS>
          Number of per-inode extent maps to cache.
          
          Each map holds one entry per EXTENT_DATA item in the file.
          
          [default: 1024]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version
